<p>Olive-oil labels may name Picual, Arbequina, Hojiblanca or Cornicabra, while others use the word <em>coupage</em>. A common misunderstanding is that monovarietal EVOO is automatically “purer” and that a blend must be a lower-grade product. <strong>That is not how olive-oil quality works.</strong></p>
<p>Monovarietal and coupage describe the varietal construction of an oil, not its legal grade or quality by themselves. A superb coupage can be more balanced and complex than a mediocre single-variety oil, while a superb monovarietal can express one cultivar with remarkable precision.</p>
<h2>What monovarietal EVOO means</h2>
<p>A monovarietal oil is made from one olive cultivar. The aim is often to show that cultivar's recognisable character: its aromatic profile, typical bitterness and pungency, structure and evolution.</p>
<p>This makes single-variety oils especially useful for learning. Tasting Picual, Arbequina or Hojiblanca separately helps you identify broad tendencies and understand how origin, harvest timing and maturity modify them.</p>
<h2>What coupage means</h2>
<p><em>Coupage</em> describes an oil made by combining oils from two or more olive cultivars. The blend may be designed to adjust aroma, intensity, bitterness, pungency, perceived sweetness, persistence or stability.</p>
<p>A serious coupage is not necessarily an improvised mixture. Producers can decide what each cultivar contributes and how the proportions create a coherent final style.</p>
<h2>A blend is not automatically lower quality</h2>
<p>Extra virgin status depends on chemical and sensory requirements, including the absence of sensory defects. It does not depend on whether one or several olive cultivars are involved. A correctly made coupage can fully meet the same extra virgin standards as a monovarietal oil.</p>
<p>The idea that “one variety is better” often comes from marketing associations between single-variety products and exclusivity. Exclusivity can be interesting, but it is not an objective quality scale.</p>
<h2>What a monovarietal is trying to express</h2>
<p>Single-variety EVOO is about <strong>cultivar identity</strong>. It allows you to study what a particular olive brings and how a producer interprets it. This is attractive for people who enjoy comparing oils or already know which cultivars they prefer.</p>
<p>A monovarietal can also have a clear connection with place when a cultivar is strongly associated with a region. Still, cultivar and terroir are not interchangeable: the same variety can taste different across farms and harvests.</p>
<h2>What a coupage is trying to achieve</h2>
<p>Blending gives the producer a design tool. One cultivar may contribute green intensity, another softer fruit, another structure or persistence. The goal can be balance or a house style that remains recognisable from year to year.</p>
<p>Some blends are deliberately versatile, designed to work both raw and in cooking. Others are intensely expressive. Coupage itself does not imply mildness.</p>
<h2>Balance is not the same as softness</h2>
<p>A balanced oil can still be intense. It may have strong fruitiness, bitterness and pungency while remaining harmonious. Balance means positive attributes fit together rather than one becoming disproportionately dominant.</p>
<p>That is why a coupage can be powerful and a monovarietal can be delicate. Neither term predicts flavour strength by itself.</p>
<h2>Harvest variation matters</h2>
<p>Even within one cultivar, earlier and later picking can produce different sensory profiles. A producer making a coupage may adjust proportions between seasons to preserve a broadly consistent style when the component oils change.</p>
<p>A monovarietal can reveal more of that year-to-year variation. For enthusiasts, observing vintage differences may be part of the appeal.</p>
<h2>Single variety does not mean identical flavour</h2>
<p>Two Picual monovarietals can taste very different. Climate, soil, harvest date, fruit condition, extraction, filtration and storage all matter. Cultivar provides a framework, not a finished recipe.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/en/picual-vs-arbequina-differences-flavour-intensity-uses/">Picual vs Arbequina comparison</a> shows why varietal descriptions should be treated as tendencies rather than rigid rules.</p>
<h2>A coupage does not erase varietal character</h2>
<p>One cultivar may dominate a blend, while another coupage is designed so no component stands out. Experienced tasters may recognise families of aromas, but reconstructing the exact formula from taste alone is not always realistic.</p>
<p>Exact proportions may not always be the most useful information for the consumer. What matters is the final sensory style and reliable information supplied by the producer.</p>
<h2>Which is better for learning about EVOO?</h2>
<p>Monovarietals are particularly educational. Tasting several cultivars separately helps build sensory memory. A side-by-side Picual and Arbequina comparison teaches more quickly than simply reading tasting notes.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/en/how-to-taste-extra-virgin-olive-oil-at-home/">home EVOO tasting guide</a> offers a simple method for comparing aroma, fruitiness, bitterness, pungency and persistence.</p>
<h2>Which is better if you want versatility?</h2>
<p>A well-designed coupage can be excellent when you want one bottle for many uses. Combining complementary characteristics can create a balanced, stable profile that integrates easily into different dishes.</p>
<p>Yet many monovarietals are equally versatile. The final sensory profile matters more than the word on the label.</p>
<h2>What else should you check?</h2>
<p>Varietal information is only one part of the purchase decision. Look at category, origin, best-before information, packaging and trustworthy harvest details. Our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-read-an-olive-oil-label/">reading an olive-oil label</a> explains which statements are legally meaningful and which are mainly marketing language.</p>
<p>Storage matters too. Light, heat and oxygen damage oil regardless of whether it is monovarietal or blended.</p>
<h2>Which is better as a gift?</h2>
<p>It depends on the recipient. A monovarietal can be appealing when you know someone's favourite cultivar or want to offer a focused tasting experience. A coupage can be excellent when you want a balanced producer-designed house style.</p>
<p>Packaging should never substitute for product quality. A premium bottle does not turn an ordinary oil into an exceptional EVOO.</p>
<h2>A simple decision guide</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Want to explore one cultivar:</strong> choose monovarietal.</li>
<li><strong>Want to compare cultivars:</strong> taste several monovarietals.</li>
<li><strong>Want a deliberately balanced house style:</strong> a good coupage can be ideal.</li>
<li><strong>Want quality:</strong> do not decide from “mono” or “coupage” alone.</li>
<li><strong>Want intensity:</strong> taste the oil; neither term guarantees it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Two philosophies, not two quality levels</h2>
<p>Monovarietal and coupage are better understood as two ways of composing an EVOO. One highlights a single cultivar's voice; the other allows a producer to create harmony from several. Both approaches can produce outstanding oils.</p>
<p>So when a label says “monovarietal”, treat it as useful compositional information rather than an automatic badge of superiority.</p>
